p if utility>=threshold. Then:\n- root: keep\n- n1: non-removable keep\n- n2: removable, utility 0.9414 >=0.55, keep\n- n3: non-removable keep\n- n4: child of n2, kept? It's removable, utility 0.0953 <0.55, so prune n4\n- n5: child of n3, non-removable keep (0.5888>=0.55 anyway)\n- n6: child of n1, removable, utility 0.9683>=0.55, keep\nSo retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n5, n6. All except n4. That seems plausible.\n\nNow compare to a-001:\nroot (1.0)\nn1 (0.58225, non-removable)\nn2 (0.44387, removable)\nn3 (0.05757, non-removable)\nn4 (0.47774, removable, child of n2)\nn5 (0.76949, non-removable, child of n3)\nn6 (0.50918, removable, child of n1)\nthreshold=0.55.\n\nApply rule:\n- root: keep\n- n1: non-removable keep (utility>=0.55 anyway)\n- n2: removable, utility 0.44387<0.55 => prune n2 and its children (including n4)\n- n3: non-removable keep (utility 0.05757<0.55 but non-removable, so keep)\n- n4: pruned because parent pruned\n- n5: child of n3, non-removable, utility 0.76949>=0.55, keep\n- n6: child of n1, removable, utility 0.50918<0.55, prune n6\nSo retained: root, n1, n3, n5. That's similar to a-000 but with different IDs.\n\nSo algorithm seems consistent: keep non-removable nodes regardless, keep removable nodes only if utility >= threshold, and if a node is pruned (removed), its entire subtree is pruned. The retained_node_ids are the ids of nodes that survive. The visit_order? Could be the order of nodes visited during a DFS pre-order of the original tree, including pruned ones? Or only visited retained? Let's think: in the pruning process, you might visit each node exactly once, decide to keep or remove. If you remove a node, you skip its subtree. So the visit_order could be the order in which nodes were visited (including those removed). In that case, for a-000, a DFS pre-order on original tree: root, n1, n6, n2, n4, n3, n5. That is a possible visit_order. But would that be expected? The output schema doesn't specify order; the visit_order could be any or